{
 "version": 1,
 "title": "The Bridge Everyone Leaned On",
 "language": "en",
 "script": [
  "Twelve thousand people once tried to watch a boat race from the same bridge. The bridge was not thrilled about it.",
  "London, eighteen seventy. Hammersmith Bridge is the first suspension bridge over the Thames, and the best free seat for the Oxford and Cambridge Boat Race.",
  "So everybody comes early. First the footpaths fill up. Then the road. Then every last gap.",
  "And when the two boats finally appear, twelve thousand people rush to the same side to see.",
  "The chains creak. The whole deck starts to sway.",
  "Luckily, the bridge held.",
  "Unluckily, the owners were watching too.",
  "You see, a bridge is built to carry weight spread out evenly. Stand in the middle, and both chains share the load. Crowd onto one side, and that side gets pushed down while the other side lifts.",
  "Ever been in a rowing boat when everyone leans over the same side? Same boat, same people, but now it tips.",
  "Twelve thousand people on one edge were doing that to a bridge.",
  "So the old chain bridge came down, and a stronger one opened on the same piers in eighteen eighty-seven.",
  "It's still standing today. But since twenty nineteen, it's been closed to cars. Cracks."
 ],
 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "In 1870 about 11,000-12,000 people packed Hammersmith Bridge to watch the Boat Race",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Hammersmith Bridge"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Bazalgette's bridge, on the same piers, opened in June 1887",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Hammersmith Bridge"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Closed to motor vehicles since 2019 after cracks were found",
   "source": "ITV News, 2026-07-01"
  }
 ],
